<blockquote>
<p>When exposed to even a very small amount of oxygen, they will engulf in flames reaching a temperature of 4000K. When this happens, they begin to run around erratically, usually burning though everything and everyone it comes close too.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why does this happen? It's not how glass works, and also like&#8230;there's oxygen in the air which means pre-containment, they'd have almost certainly been on fire constantly.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Due to the severe flammability, all methods that foundation has thus far have been found not heat resistant enough, hence ineffective.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>why don't they just keep her in a nitrogen atmosphere? are they concerned she might asphyxiate because that doesn't seem like it'd be an issue between 'no bodily orifices' and 'burns up all the oxygen around her'?</p>
<hr />
<p>I guess at the end of the day, my biggest issue with this is it just&#8230;doesn't feel very interesting to read: it's kinda just a generic 'super keter uncontainable monster' idea with a story very focussed on recontainment and to me that whole angle is a lot less interesting than a much more personal story about a super fragile woman made of glass who spends her whole life terrified of being shattered or chipped, and like, really exploring the toll that like a series of small breakages has on her mental condition; and the foundation adjusting containment to try and prevent that but in a way that like&#8230;increasingly coddles her so like, she might not break but she can't like&#8230;watch tv or anything in case she damages herself on the remote or w/e, y'know?</p>

